    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870–1871 brought down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a>. After his capture at the Battle of Sedan on 1 September 1870, Parisian deputies led by Léon Gambetta set up the Government of National Defence on 4 September, with General Louis-Jules Trochu as its president. It ruled through the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and Gambetta left the city in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hot-air-balloon">hot air balloon</a> to establish the government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. After the surrender of January 1871, a conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a> head of the executive and signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 10 May 1871, ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><p>In Paris, workers and National Guards rose and took power as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, which the army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon">Patrice de MacMahon</a> crushed in the Bloody Week of May 1871. The monarchist majority agreed on the Comte de Chambord as king, but he would not accept the tricolour flag, so a "temporary" republic was set up. The constitutional laws of February 1875 created a president, a Chamber of Deputies and a Senate. After MacMahon's crisis of 16 May 1877, republicans won both houses, and in 1879 he resigned, ending the prospect of a restoration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was a rural nation, and the peasant farmer was the typical citizen. Eugen Weber argued in Peasants into Frenchmen that railroads, republican schools and conscription gave rural France a sense of national identity by about 1900, though some scholars hold it existed before 1870. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> led a new consumer culture: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> stores such as Le Bon Marché and La Samaritaine sold at fixed prices, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a> set Au Bonheur des Dames in one. The daily press of Paris grew from 1 million copies in 1870 to 5 million in 1910, though banks and foreign governments secretly paid papers for favourable coverage.</p><p>The welfare state lagged behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>: an accident insurance law came in 1898 and a voluntary national pension plan in 1910. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> caused damage of about 113% of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> of 1913, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> rose from 66% of GDP to 170% in 1919. The economy prospered from 1926 to 1929 after Raymond Poincaré balanced the budget. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> came late, around 1931, and was relatively mild. In 1936 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> brought a 40-hour week and two weeks of paid holiday.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rebuilt its army, with new artillery and, after 1905, heavy investment in military aircraft. Its colonial armies fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> of 1884–1885, in which Admiral Courbet destroyed the Chinese fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, and suppressed rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1925, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1926 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> in 1930. In 1913 military service was extended from two years to three.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Anglo-French victory at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> in September 1914 defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s plan for a quick win, and the war became one of trench warfare and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>, reaching its height at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a>; 1.5 million French soldiers died. By 1918 France produced more munitions and artillery than its allies. Between the wars it built the Maginot Line along the German border. In 1939 the commander in chief, Maurice Gamelin, saw little action in the Phoney War beyond the Saar Offensive. In May 1940 he sent his best divisions north under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dyle</a> Plan, while German forces crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">River Meuse</a> near Sedan. The historian Julian Jackson holds that the plan was destined for failure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic had a president as head of state, a directly elected Chamber of Deputies and an indirectly elected Senate, with a ministry under the President of the Council. When Jules Grévy succeeded MacMahon in 1879 he promised not to use the power of dissolution, which made the system parliamentary until 1940. Governing coalitions rarely lasted more than a few months; the journalist John Gunther counted 103 cabinets with an average length of eight months.</p><p>Politics were sharply polarized between a reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> heir to the Revolution and a conservative France rooted in the peasantry, the Church and the army. Republicans weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>: the Ferry laws of 1881 and 1882 made schools secular, Émile Combes closed parochial schools and banned the religious orders, and the law of 1905 separated Church and State. The Radical Party, founded in 1901, was the most important party of the early 20th century, and the Democratic Republican Alliance became the main centre-right party. The republic survived the Boulanger crisis of 1889, the Panama scandals of 1892, the Dreyfus affair and the riot of 6 February 1934, when police killed 15 demonstrators.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the expansionist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry</a> the republic acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, vast territories in West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The colonies were ruled under the claim of a civilizing mission, and Catholic missionaries worked to convert their peoples. By 1939 a third of French exports went to the colonies; settlers' land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> grew from 1,600,000 hectares in 1890 to 2,700,000 in 1940. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, occupied the Saar Basin, shared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s African colonies such as Kamerun with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and took the Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> its diplomacy moved from isolation in 1871 to the centre of the alliance system in 1914, through the Franco-Russian Alliance of 1894 and the Entente Cordiale of 1904 with Britain. The Dreyfus affair, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>'s J'accuse exposed the army's case against a Jewish officer, became a universal symbol of injustice. The republic was the first stable republican government in French history and the first to win majority support, and the Fourth Republic that followed was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a> not unlike it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The interwar years were marked by strife between right and left. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sought allies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> but found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> unwilling to commit and its Eastern European allies weak. When Germany remilitarized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> on 7 March 1936, nothing was done, and in 1938 Édouard Daladier signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a>, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> to Nazi Germany. Rearmament, begun in 1936, bore fruit only in 1939 and 1940.</p><p>After Germany invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Britain and France declared war in 1939. Paul Reynaud replaced Daladier in March 1940, and after the German breakthrough of May and June he refused to support an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> and resigned on 16 June. On 10 July 1940 the French parliament gave full powers to Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a>, who proclaimed the French State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> called on the French to rally to Free France. Vichy never formally abolished the republic, which, in Jackson's words, "surreptitiously disappeared". Historians have long debated whether the collapse reflected a national "decadence", as Marc Bloch and Jean-Baptiste Duroselle argued, or military and structural causes, as Robert J. Young and those who followed him argued.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The French Third Republic was born of defeat in 1870 and meant to be temporary, yet it became the longest-lasting French regime since 1789. It secularized schools and separated Church and State, built the second largest colonial empire and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, but its unstable coalitions could not meet Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and it ended with the Fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940.</em></p>

